A tiny signal-style counter (SolidJS-flavored) shows the mechanic directly — no `setState`, no component re-render, just a subscriber getting notified:

```jsx
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = createSignal(0);

  // This function body runs ONCE, at setup.
  return (
    <button onClick={() => setCount(count() + 1)}>
      {count()}   {/* this expression re-runs on its own when count changes */}
    </button>
  );
}
```

Calling `setCount` doesn't re-invoke `Counter` — it notifies exactly the one place that read `count()`, which is the text expression inside the button, and only that DOM text node updates. Compare this to the equivalent React `useState` counter: clicking the button re-runs the entire `Counter` function body from top to bottom, and React relies on the virtual-DOM diff afterward to notice that only the text changed.
